WEBVTT

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.440
The variety of decisions you can make during a task has reduced a

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.800
lot over the years and now you have these massive gaggles and

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.280
very fast tasks going along ridges and this has led to a big

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:24.640
increase in the risks taken and much less variation in the way

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.680
that pilots can fly the task. There's two big problems with

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.560
the drag noodles. The first problem is that really

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.640
they're only talking about the weight range of 100 to 125

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.800
kilos, whereas I'm trying to compensate for people who only

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.160
weigh 40 kilos to do competitions as well.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.960
MRT is multi radius turn points So what it means is that if you

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.160
are a lighter weight pilot then you can have a bigger radius and

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.280
this negates the performance advantage which those guys have.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.400
Hello, and welcome back to Paragliding Atlas.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.040
I'm your host, Aninda Singh, and joining me today is a man who

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.200
has won many hats. From designing some of the most

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.360
pioneering gliders to revamping the scoring system, Bruce

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.080
Goldsmith is coming out with solutions one way or the other.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.240
Bruce, welcome to the show. Thanks very much.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.120
Very good to be here. Bruce, I want to get going right

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.760
away because past couple of weeks we have seen quite a lot

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.080
of intense discussions on various solutions that are being

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.000
offered in the world of para lighting and scoring seems to be

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.320
a big pain point in one of them. And I'm pretty sure the

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.600
audiences that are tuning in can relate to our previous episode

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.640
where Luke clearly mentioned that the way designers put out

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.840
their designs in the gliders, a lot of it depends on how the

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.320
scoring is carried out. So just to get started, why

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.720
haven't we been able to figure out scoring even after three or

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.000
four decades of modern day paragliding?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.160
Well, I think that the goal posts have been moving a lot,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.800
you know, so things have changed and the way tasks are done has

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.200
changed a lot. I mean, when I first started

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.720
doing competitions, it was all about open distance.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.200
And the first competitions I was doing, we had open distance

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.800
every day. So we just fly as far as you

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.040
could every day. And that was a very famous

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.560
competition in in Como in Northern Italy.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.080
And so the whole thing was based on on on distance flying and

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.840
flying far. But now it's all about racing.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.360
So the goal posts have completely switched everything.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.480
Everything has changed. So from distance to speed.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.040
Bruce, a lot of times it's said that the way we chase glory at

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.160
the highest level is somehow incentivizing risk over tactics.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.960
Your goal post change analogy, Do you think somehow we have

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.680
completely moved away from the fact that there was a time when

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.800
we were chasing smarter decision making and right now it's all

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.880
about who can go out there and deal with the most amount of

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.560
turbulence or mayhem? Yeah, I think that the variety

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.760
of decisions you can make during a task has reduced a lot over

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.240
the years. And now you have these massive

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.280
gaggles and very fast tasks going along ridges and this has

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:41.040
led to a big increase in the risks taken and much less

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.440
variation in the way the pilots can fly the task.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:51.160
So let's say when it comes to compensating the field, we have

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.600
struggled with the question of ballast for quite a long time

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.480
where size has pretty much defined on how your glide will

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.440
be or what your performance will be.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.160
So you have seen the industry evolve for more than 4 decades I

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.080
believe, you know, be as as as a racer yourself or as a designer

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.520
or somebody who kind of goes behind the legislation.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:22.520
Can we hope for an eventual solution in near future or is

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:27.880
this a debatable topic that will more or less reach a stalemate

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.200
because the opinions will always differ and we humans will find

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.160
it hard to come to consensus for when it comes to chasing glory

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.000
and and deciding who's the best in the sky.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.000
Well, I think part of the problem is the way the rules

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
have been written that people have actually been ignoring this

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.640
issue of size, and the size problem has become worse and

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.040
worse over the years. One of the classic problems is

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.120
the problem of the load test for gliders.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.440
Normally most manufacturers, they load test the large size

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.160
and then put the large lines on the smaller size gliders, which

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.800
obviously increases the performance advantage for big

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.160
gliders. And also the way that the tasks

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.960
have been run and the the way, the the way that pilots and

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.000
competitions always go to the strongest places in the world at

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.080
the strongest time of the year. Before we used to often do

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:30.440
competitions in weak conditions. And when we have when you have

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.640
weak conditions, then the lightweight pilots have an

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.520
advantage. But nowadays they just cancel

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.680
the tasks if conditions are weak or they just go to the strongest

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.200
places in the world at the strongest time of the year.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.040
So as this, yeah, as this has become a growing problem over

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.920
the over the years, I've been trying over the last 8 to 10

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.200
years to come up with a solution to solve this issue.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.080
And so we ran the waiters competition where we were using

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.880
the competition and all the pilots as Guinea pigs to try

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.880
different kinds of systems to see what we could do to overcome

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.840
this problem of the big weight advantage that people have.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.560
OK, Just sort of curiosity because Weightless and MRT have

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.040
been thrown around quite a lot in the in the discussion forums

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.840
the past few days. Are these the same branches of

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.000
one solution or are these two completely different things that

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.800
that we're talking about here? MRT being the multiple radius

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.320
term vote for our audiences. Yeah, Weightless was was was a

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.880
series of competitions which I set up to investigate ways in

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.720
which weight would not count in competitions.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.400
So that was the competition. And we started by having weight

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.600
classes, which is the obvious solution.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.640
And so we had three weight classes.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.840
They were up to 8080 to 100 and above 100 kilos.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:59.400
And we ran the first competition like this and we actually found

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.960
it made it worse, not better, because part of the idea is that

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.480
people don't need to carry ballast.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.840
And so when you have those weight classes, everybody just

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.880
ballasted up to the top of their class.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.080
So it just ended up with everybody carrying lots of

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.280
ballast. So it was exactly the opposite

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.200
of the effect we wanted. I'm going to quickly compare

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.240
this to the drag noodles that have been making waves in

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.960
discussion forums as well. From from your perspective, of

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.760
course, you know they look ugly. They are negating the fact that

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.440
we wanted to reduce drag in the 1st place and a lot of pilots

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.040
are opposed to it. From your point of view, is this

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.040
matching that solution or is this better or is this falling

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.040
short? What do you think when we are

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:52.440
comparing these two solutions? Yeah, for me there's two big

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:59.120
problems with the drag noodles. The first problem is that really

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.200
they're only talking about the weight range of 100 to 125

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.360
kilos, whereas I'm trying to compensate for people who only

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.360
weigh 40 kilos to do competitions as well, you know.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.800
So we're talking about the full range of pilots.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:20.320
And when you look at the weight range of say 100 to 125 kilos,

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.640
the performance difference is about 5%.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.880
So this you can manage to to adjust with noodles.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.960
But when you're talking about the difference between say 65

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.600
kilos and 125 kilos, so that's the difference between an extra

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:38.039
small to an extra large, then you're talking more like 20%.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.840
And Dragnoodles, there's no way you can compensate 20% of

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.640
performance difference with the dragon noodles.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.800
To make it even more simpler, you know, I'm just trying to

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:51.680
break down the whole thing in a way that we all can fathom that

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.120
what's exactly is going on. So let's say on a scale of 1 to

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.200
10, if you have to see the effectiveness of current scoring

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.640
system that is being employed in FAI or CAT1, where do you see

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.320
where would you rate the current scoring system and then the BGD

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.640
weightless class where, where would that come in, in

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.880
comparison if we are just talking about numbers and then

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.000
we will dive into the details that where you know we are

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.560
lacking or we are better off in in one way or the other.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.840
Well, my. Idea was not actually to start

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.400
at the top level. You know, we're told the, the

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.640
weightless competition is a friendly pilots competition with

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:37.160
intermediate gliders flying it's been C Class or C and up to C&D

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:42.600
class and it's not open class CCC competition.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:50.840
But what it does is that it enables pilots who fly extra

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.880
small gliders to actually compete along with the

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.320
heavyweight pilots. And that is, you know, a massive

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.920
eye opener for for those pilots because normally they can't even

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.240
be in the air in the same air at the same time.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.760
You know, it's such a such a huge difference.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.400
Perhaps we should talk about actually what MRT is because we

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:18.320
haven't really explained it. I said by first competition we

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.960
did was with weight classes and then after that we went over to

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:28.160
a handicap system and then after that we tried MRT.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.720
So MRT is multi radius turn points.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.720
So what it means is that a typical turn point has say a 400

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.080
metre radius. If you look at the image in

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.920
front of you, that would be say a 400 metre radius.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.680
And then you have if you're if you're a lighter weight pilot,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.040
then you can have a bigger radius and that means that you

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.680
fly a task which is a shorter distance.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.240
So that means it gives you a little advantage over the big

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.800
guy. So this just compensates for the

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.920
advantage the big guy already has.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.680
So you end up at each turn point with a small boost for the small

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.920
pilots. And it's actually quite

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:14.000
interesting to see this when it's actually happening during a

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.720
competition, You know, because what happens is that the

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.920
lightweight pilots tend to get left behind.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:23.480
And then as you get to a turn point, you all come in together

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:26.160
with the lightweight pilots behind and you turn around and

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.440
then you find yourself back in line with the lightweight pilots

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.880
again and you haven't left them behind.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.440
So it's quite interesting to see it when it's actually in use in

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:39.440
a competition. Bruce, I want to dive into one

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:42.600
of these WhatsApp chats that have been going on in the pilots

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.920
union that has recently formed. And quite a few pilots has their

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:54.120
own feelings about MRT. And Max Pinot said that modern

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.840
tactics do not go well with this.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.480
If you're able to see my screen, I'm going to just zoom in here.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:05.960
And it's said that we are negating the the tactics over

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:10.160
here and by shortening you are making those pilots, exposing

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:15.360
them to counter attacks. But more than that, something

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:22.200
that really caught my eye is that if Stan clearly mentioned

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:28.360
that if we are turning before the trigger, because he said

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:34.360
that a lot of thermal sources is where the waypoint is placed by

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.920
the task committee. So if small pilots might be

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.280
turning before, then they do have a disadvantage.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.440
So let's try and visualize that in this image.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.040
And is there a way forward? Or, you know, it's like, yeah,

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:51.120
we can't really please everyone, but at least it gives us more

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.200
possibilities. Well, it's actually very easy to

00:12:55.320 --> 00:13:01.200
answer that that point. I mean, you know, thermals are

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.000
in some places and not in other places.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.480
And sometimes when you're going to a turn point, you might need

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.280
to go a bit further to find the thermal and sometimes it can go

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:14.200
the other way. You know, you find a thermal and

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.880
then the others have to go out of it.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.480
So what I mean, it seems to be that what Stan is saying is that

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.960
if you have a longer task, this can be an advantage.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:26.840
But clearly this is nonsense. You know, the shorter task, the

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.840
quicker it is to fly. It's very easy.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:33.320
You don't have to be a genius. So in the end, you have whatever

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:38.160
a task of 80 kilometres, you have an an average speed and the

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.960
average time it takes you to do that task, if you do 100

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.640
kilometres, it's going to take you longer, you know, simple as

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:45.880
that. And and you know, you gain some,

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.680
you lose some, but in the end everything averages out.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:55.280
So I would say this is a really a false criticism of the system.

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:58.720
Fair enough. I would.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.760
I would want to move back to weightless for a bit.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.640
Would you like to explain the weightless concept and how it

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:10.320
basically started and how it has eventually evolved to MRT?

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.800
And maybe you know how it goes further?

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.920
Can I quickly go back to the last, the last messages that

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:24.360
it's interesting to note that they were coming from, well, 2

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.000
out of 3 is coming from big pilots, you know, very large

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.520
pilots. And there's obviously a very big

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:33.120
resistance from the large pilots to lose the advantage which they

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:37.320
have, you know, so that's, you know, clearly has to be said,

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:41.040
you know that it's large pilots have an advantage and they don't

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.560
want to lose it. Simple as that.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:50.360
So going on going on to the the MRT system itself, we just ran

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:52.880
the last version of the MRT. You know the MRT is actually

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.600
changing every year. Every year when we run a new

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:59.600
competition we put in new technology and try new things

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.520
and try and improve the system all the time.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:07.960
At the last competition, what actually happened was we weigh

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:12.080
all the pilots at the registration, then we have 10

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.000
weight classes which everybody fits in.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:22.480
And then when they set the task, you set the task for the longest

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.000
task, which is the heaviest weight task.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:31.440
And then the scoring system calculates how much shorter all

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:33.840
the other ten groups need to fly.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:37.960
And those calculations are all based on statistics.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:42.480
They're just based on analysing previous competitions and the

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.320
performance of different weight gliders.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:51.600
So you can you can actually analyse this pretty closely and

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.200
the more competitions we run, the more accurate will be this

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.160
compensation. And so then all the pilots take

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:03.000
off. The start and finish are not

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:07.160
MRT, they're just standard starts and finishes and then the

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:14.520
task, all the turn points during the event, then MRT and, and

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:18.480
then you, you finish at the end. And the nice thing is, is that

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:24.320
because the lighter pilots are able to fly a shorter distance

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:26.760
that they can actually win the task.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:31.520
Whereas with with any other system of handicaps, for

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.880
example, the lighter guys won't actually be the first guy to

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.760
cross the line, which is why we won't a handicap is, is you

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:42.920
just, everybody flies the same task and then you just give

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:45.760
somebody more points at the end because of their weight.

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:50.320
And that's what they use in sailing, you know, so you just,

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:54.240
but it means that they are not up at the front of the

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:58.040
competition leading the task. You know, it means that they are

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:01.000
far behind and then they get a boost to their points.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.480
But it's, you know, it's a boost to the points, but it's not the

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:07.839
best way. You know, it's, it's, it doesn't

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:09.599
give them the excitement of winning the race.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.440
So that's the whole idea that even a light person can get the

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:17.240
excitement of winning the race. Do you think this is going to

