00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:32.560
Bruce, glider sizes have been in question for quite a while and

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:36.800
more or less the bigger the better has always remained the

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.000
norm. So when we look at performance

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:44.400
differences, what's your view on that topic and how can that be

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:47.120
equalised, if at all in future? Yeah.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:50.720
Well, I asked the same question a few years ago and I had an

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:54.840
engineer, Rene Falcier from Switzerland, who worked on this

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.960
exact topic for me. And he was able to analyse the

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:01.800
difference between an extra small glider to extra large

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:06.480
glider and precisely calculate all the differences in

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:09.840
performance between extra small to extra large.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:15.760
And the overall conclusion he came to was that there was a 6%

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:21.880
improvement in glide ratio, 5% higher top speed for the extra

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:27.600
large and a 25% improvement in dynamic reactions.

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:33.480
So that's absolutely huge. But I mean, what are dynamic

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:37.320
reactions? I mean, dynamic reactions is the

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:39.800
way that the glider reacts to turbulence.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:45.000
And so you have to remember that if you have a chunk of

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:48.200
turbulence and you have somebody with an extra large glider,

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:51.520
let's say that the the turbulent chunk of turbulence is the same

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:55.200
size as the extra large glider, you know, and then the so the

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:57.920
extra large glider will lose a bit of height because he might

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:01.840
make some pitch movements, might even get a collapse or whatever

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:05.280
and then carry on flying. Let's say he loses 10 meters.

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:08.440
Then if you've got the extra small glider going into the same

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:11.320
piece of turbulence, that turbulence is double the size

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:15.120
for him, double the size. So it's going to affect him a

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:18.360
lot more. And as I said, the calculation

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:24.120
is that it actually effects 25% more for the extra small glider

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.920
compared to the extra large glider.

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:30.880
So when you're considering the performance improvement, you

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:34.360
have to look at all those three things, glide, performance,

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:38.960
speed and dynamic effects. And that's why it's not just a

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:42.920
few small percent. It's actually much larger than

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:46.480
just the performance difference. So in short, if you're just

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:49.000
going out and chasing cross country numbers then the bigger

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.560
the better is always the solution and you can't challenge

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:55.960
that no matter what. Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.560
Yeah. I mean, personally, I've put on

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:03.800
10 kilos recently and I can feel an enormous improvement in the

